  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nExpect mostly quiet weather conditions with surface high pressure \npushing east across the region. Diurnal cumulus has developed across \nmuch of central Indiana as convective temperatures have been \nreached. The greatest coverage is across western portions of central \nIndiana where there is slightly deeper moisture present. Look for \nthe diurnal cumulus to dissipate during the evening hours. Mostly \nclear skies and very light winds could promote additional patchy fog \ndevelopment late tonight. Drying soils the past few afternoons will \nkeep coverage more limited, but cannot rule out fog in rural areas \nor near rivers. Any fog that develops will mix out in typical \ndiurnal fashion Wednesday morning. \n\nExpect surface high pressure to continue shifting east on Wednesday \nwhile a shortwave aloft and associated weak surface low approach \nvery late in the day. Residual subsidence induced dry air will keep \nweather conditions dry through the day, but a stray light shower \nappears possible near far northwestern counties during the evening. \nExpect temperatures to gradually warm thanks to modest warm air \nadvection. Highs are generally going to be near normal. \n\nWednesday night through early next week...\n\nGuidance depicts a longwave trough across the north-central CONUS \nduring the remainder of the forecast period with multiple embedded \nshortwaves traversing the region. These impulses will promote \nadditional convective chances at times as deeper moisture surges \nback into central Indiana. The greatest precipitation chances are on \nThursday and Friday when the strongest forcing overlaps with the \ndeeper moisture surging northward. A frontal boundary associated \nwith a weak disturbance will likely stall near the region by \nThursday. Additional shortwaves tracking along the boundary supports \ndaily precipitation chances through early next week.\n\nSome guidance suggest low amplitude upper ridging builds in Sunday \ninto Monday with an EML advecting towards the region. This leads to \nlower confidence in convective coverage and precipitation chances \nboth days. Varying model solutions also results in higher \nuncertainty regarding specific details on timing or evolution of \neach subsequent shortwave pushing into central Indiana. \n\nIn general, models depict strong destabilization most days and \nenhancement of mid-upper level flow from the passing waves. This \ncould potentially support isolated severe thunderstorms somewhere \nnear the region, but expect confidence to remain low until models \nbecome better aligned. Some guidance is already hinting at MCSs or \nstorm clusters developing upstream and potentially propagating \ntowards central Indiana at times this weekend into early next week. \nLocalized flooding from repeated rounds of convection and isolated \ndamaging wind gusts would be the primary threats if this scenario \noccurs.",
